{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786281535,"nonce":"uzAQLAfkz09GHrnZfSEHF0R8VAM=","title":"**What is wrong.** SPEC §C.6 and §C.7 each have **two independent implementations** in","message":"the tree, and in both pairs the one in the shared kernel is the dead one:\n\n- §C.7 idempotency: `internal/alerts/domain.ComputeIdempotencyKey` has **no production\n  caller**. `internal/notification/domain.IdempotencyKey` is the one `notify.go`\n  actually calls.\n- §C.6 rule fingerprint: `internal/alerts/domain` and `internal/rules/domain.Fingerprint`\n  both implement it, over different input shapes.\n\nUntil `c133981` nothing asserted the two members of either pair agreed. They agreed by\nluck. `c133981` added cross-check tests (`TestFingerprintAgreesWithTheKernel`,\n`TestIdempotencyKeyAgreesWithTheKernel`) over 400 input tuples, so the divergence is now\ncaught — but the duplication itself remains, and so does a dead exported function in the\nkernel that a reader will reasonably assume is the canonical one.\n\n**Why it matters.** CONTEXT.md §5.2b makes `internal/alerts/domain` the shared domain\nkernel and the single sanctioned cross-domain `domain` import — precisely so identity\ncomputation has one home. A kernel function that looks canonical, is exported, is\ntested, and is called by nothing is a trap: the next person to touch identity will edit\nit, see green tests, and change nothing that ships. That is exactly the shape\n`tools/lintreach` exists to find, and `lintreach` does not flag it because the function\ndoes have callers — in tests.\n\n`c133981` had to fix the framing in **four** places for one spec clause family. That\ncost is recurring, not one-off.\n\n**Why the duplication exists.** `rules/domain` receives a recovered rule's labels as a\nraw map that has never passed `NewLabels` and may not satisfy the label-name charset, so\nit cannot simply call the kernel. That is a real constraint, not laziness, and any fix\nhas to address it rather than assume it away.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/domain/keys.go` (`ComputeIdempotencyKey`,\n`ComputeRuleFingerprint`), `internal/notification/domain/idempotency.go`,\n`internal/rules/domain/snapshot.go` (`Canon`, `Fingerprint`).\n\n**Done when.** Each of §C.6 and §C.7 has one implementation, or the SPEC names which of\nthe pair is canonical and why the other must exist, and the dead kernel function is\neither deleted or given the production caller its position implies.","files":null}]}